<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paderborn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paderborn is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the springs of the Pader. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> held assemblies there from 777 and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> there in 799, and it became the seat of a bishopric, later a prince-bishopric that lasted until 1802. Its cathedral and the remains of its Carolingian palace recall this history, and it is home to a university founded in 1972.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Paderborn lies in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and is the capital of the Paderborn district. Its name comes from the river Pader and Born, an old German term for the source of a river, and the Pader rises from more than 200 springs near Paderborn Cathedral, where Saint Liborius is buried. The city is about 50 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> and 104 km east of Dortmund, with the hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egge-hills">Eggegebirge</a> to the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Paderborn was founded as a bishopric by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in 795, though its official history began in 777, when he built a castle near the springs. In 799 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> fled his enemies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and reached Paderborn, where he met Charlemagne and stayed for three months. It was during this time that it was decided that Charlemagne would be crowned emperor, and Leo crowned him in Rome in 800. In 836 Saint Liborius became the city's patron saint after Bishop Badurad moved his bones from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>. The bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meinwerk">Meinwerk</a> became a Prince of the Empire in 1100, and the area became a place for the emperors to stay.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took the city in 1802, then the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> held it from 1807 to 1813 before it returned to Prussia. In 1804 Friedrich Sertürner, a pharmacist's apprentice, was the first to isolate morphine from opium. In 1914 the military camp became the Sennelager prisoner of war camp, and the see became an archdiocese in 1930. Allied bombing in 1944 and 1945 destroyed 85 per cent of the city, including many historic buildings, and the US 3rd Armored Division seized it after a battle on 31 March and 1 April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Paderborn has a population of over 155,000, about 10 per cent of them students at Paderborn University. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> founded a university there in 1614, which closed in 1819 and was re-founded in 1972. After reconstruction in the 1940s and 1950s the city became a major industrial seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, and it is the home of Nixdorf Computer, whose museum, the Heinz Nixdorf MuseumsForum, opened in 1996 and is described as the largest computer museum in the world. Its Kaiserpfalz and Diocesan Museum recall its early history, and the British Army kept a significant presence in the area until 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paderborn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saxony was then governed by Frankish counts and bishops, and Christianity was spread through new sees such as <b>Paderborn</b>, Bremen and Hildesheim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Saxony was then organised into counties and bishoprics such as <b>Paderborn</b>, Münster and Bremen, and later formed the Duchy of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship developed between the bishoprics of <b>Paderborn</b> and Minden.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lippe">County of Lippe</a></li><li><blockquote>A branch of the counts of Arnsberg established itself at Rietberg on the upper Ems around 1237, founding a small county in the marshy lowlands between the territories of Münster, <b>Paderborn</b> and Ravensberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-rietberg">County of Rietberg</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne held assemblies at <b>Paderborn</b> from 777 and met Pope Leo III there in 799, the prelude to his imperial coronation.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-paderborn">Prince-Bishopric of Paderborn</a></li></ul></section>
